# Project: AI Accessibility Narrator

## Product truth
This is an audio-first assistive workflow, not a generic chatbot.
The primary action is "listen", not "type a prompt".

## Stack
- Frontend: Next.js App Router + TypeScript
- Backend: Python + FastAPI
- Captioning: Salesforce BLIP checkpoint (pin exact checkpoint/license)
- VQA: BLIP VQA checkpoint / compatible local runtime
- TTS: Piper with a pinned voice model
- Web extraction: Playwright + BeautifulSoup
- OCR stretch: Tesseract
- Local orchestration: Docker Compose

## Privacy rules
- No third-party AI API in the MVP.
- Never upload image bytes to OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, or any other inference service.
- Do not log raw image data.
- The URL may be fetched over the network; document this separately from local AI inference.

## UX rules
- Do not turn the primary UI into chat bubbles.
- Narration stage is primary.
- Question box is secondary and tied to the current session.
- Segment/sentence highlighting is required.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion.

## Webpage rules
- Use structural semantics rather than raw text soup.
- Exclude scripts, styles, hidden content and repeated chrome.
- Preserve heading hierarchy.
- Include meaningful alt text.
- Expose parser errors rather than guessing.

## Audio rules
- MVP sync is segment-level.
- Generate one audio clip per segment.
- Do not attempt word-level highlighting until segment-level sync is reliable.

## Accessibility rules
- Semantic HTML first.
- Keyboard navigation is mandatory.
- Every control has an accessible name.
- Never use color as the only state indicator.
- Test with NVDA before demo.

## AI rules
- Do not invent visual details that are not supported by the image.
- Prefer "appears", "may", or "I can't determine" when uncertain.
- Keep VQA context tied to the active image/session.
- Keep impairment preview explicitly non-clinical.

## Definition of done
1. Image -> narration works locally.
2. Follow-up VQA -> spoken answer works.
3. Webpage -> ordered segments + read-along works.
4. Keyboard + screen-reader QA passes core flows.
5. Network inspection confirms no third-party AI image upload during the local demo.
